<section class="mt-7" aria-label="Why hearts and wearables care">
  <p class="mt-7 leading-relaxed text-white/80">A heart monitor or wearable can look at an <strong class="text-white">ECG</strong>—a trace of the heart’s electrical activity. A heartbeat creates a brief burst of activity, followed by quieter stretches. The signal is not equally busy every moment.</p>
  <div class="mt-6 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
    <article class="rounded-2xl border border-white/10 bg-white/[0.035] p-5 sm:p-6">
      <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.16em] text-neutral">Conventional neural networks</p>
      <h2 class="mt-2 font-serif text-xl font-bold text-white">Keep calculating</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-white/75">Many conventional AI designs keep processing values at each step, even when the input is changing very little. On a small, battery-powered device, that steady activity can be a power challenge.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="rounded-2xl border border-primary/30 bg-primary/[0.07] p-5 sm:p-6">
      <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.16em] text-primary">Spiking neural networks</p>
      <h2 class="mt-2 font-serif text-xl font-bold text-white">Listen for a change</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-white/75">SNNs take inspiration from how biological neurons communicate with brief electrical pulses, or “spikes.” An event-driven design can stay quieter between meaningful changes—but actual power savings depend on how the network is built and measured on hardware.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
  <p class="mt-4 rounded-xl border-l-2 border-primary px-4 py-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-white/70">That difference is the idea, not a result claim: this project still needs real hardware measurements before anyone can say how much energy it uses.</p>
</section>
